problems.problem:
Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ which satisfy the equality
$$f(x + f(y)) = f(x - f(y)) + 4xf(y)$$
for any $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$. (Here $\mathbb{R}$ denotes the set of real numbers.)

Find the maximum number of sets which simultaneously satisfy the following conditions:

i) any of the sets consists of $4$ elements;

ii) any two different sets have exactly $2$ common elements;

iii) no two elements are common to all the sets.

Do there exist integers $m$, $n$ and a function $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying simultaneously the following two conditions (here $\mathbb{R}$ denotes the set of real numbers):

i) $f(f(x)) = 2f(x) - x - 2$ for any $x \in \mathbb{R}$;

ii) $m \le n$ and $f(m) = n$?

Let $U = \{1, 2, \ldots, 2014\}$. For positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$ we denote by $f(a, b, c)$ the number of ordered 6-tuples of sets $(X_1, X_2, X_3, Y_1, Y_2, Y_3)$ satisfying the following conditions:

(i) $Y_1 \subseteq X_1 \subseteq U$ and $|X_1| = a$;

(ii) $Y_2 \subseteq X_2 \subseteq U \setminus Y_1$ and $|X_2| = b$;

(iii) $Y_3 \subseteq X_3 \subseteq U \setminus (Y_1 \cup Y_2)$ and $|X_3| = c$.

Prove that $f(a, b, c)$ does not change when $a$, $b$, $c$ are rearranged.

Let $P(x)$ be a nonconstant polynomial of degree $n$ with rational coefficients which can not be presented as a product of two nonconstant polynomials with rational coefficients. Prove that the number of polynomials $Q(x)$ of degree less than $n$ with rational coefficients such that $P(x)$ divides $P(Q(x))$

a) is finite;

b) does not exceed $n$.
